      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the probability that the two-digit number \\(ab\\) (where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are the results of two dice rolls and both numbers must be divisible by 3) and both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are divisible by 3, we first identify the favorable outcomes.\n\nThe numbers on a fair 6-sided die are 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, and 6. The numbers that are divisible by 3 are 3 and 6. Therefore, the possible values for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are 3 and 6.\n\nWe need to look at all the pairs \\((a, b)\\) where both numbers can be 3 or 6, giving us the pairs:\n- (3, 3)\n- (3, 6)\n- (6, 3)\n- (6, 6)\n\nThe total number of outcomes is \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\). So out of 36 possible outcomes, there are 4 favorable outcomes. The probability is calculated by dividing the number of favorable outcomes by the total number of possible outcomes.\n\nThus, the probability is given by:\n\n\\[\nP = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{4}{36} = \\frac{1}{9}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{9}}\\).\n \n \n To solve this math problem, why didn't you just say 4/6?\n\nYou'd need to count those four favorable outcomes out , and derectly use:\n\\[ \\frac{4}{36} = \\frac{1}{9} \\]\ntheres no need to count those 4 specific two-digit combinations.\n\nIf you want it all at once,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{9}\n\\]\n@above\n[EN] Congratulations for posting. This is a starting point for you.\n[/EN]\n@below $36$\n$\\rlap//\\/\\/$\\boxed{\\frac{1}{9}}",
