      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the probability that both numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), obtained from the outcomes of two fair 6-sided dice, are divisible by 3. Let's break down the solution step-by-step:\n\n1. **Identify the total number of possible outcomes:**\n   Each die has 6 faces, so the total number of outcomes when two dice are tossed is:\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 6 = 36\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the favorable outcomes where both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are divisible by 3:**\n   The numbers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) must both be 3 or 6 (since these are the only multiples of 3 on a 6-sided die) and can be selected in any of the following combinations with any other digit from 1 to 6:\n   - \\(a = 3\\) and \\(b = 3\\)\n   - \\(a = 3\\) and \\(b = 6\\)\n   - \\(a = 6\\) and \\(b = 3\\)\n   - \\(a = 6\\) and \\(b = 6\\)\n\n3. **Count the number of successful outcomes:**\n   There are 4 favorable outcomes:\n   - \\((3, 3)\\)\n   - \\((3, 6)\\)\n   - \\((6, 3)\\)\n   - \\((6, 6)\\)\n\n4. **Calculate the probability:**\n   The probability \\(P\\) that both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are divisible by 3 is the ratio of the number of successful outcomes to the total number of possible outcomes:\n   \\[\n   P = \\frac{\\text{Number of favorable outcomes}}{\\text{Total number of outcomes}} = \\frac{4}{36} = \\frac{1}{9}\n   \\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{9}}\n\\]",
